  "summary": "Massive teak doors with brass studs, carved rosettes and inscriptions guarding Swahili houses.",
  "description": "The carved door (mlangi) announced a Swahili house's standing: colossal teak or mahogany leaves — some a ton each — framed in lintels carved with lotus buds, fish, chains and Quranic inscriptions, studded with brass or iron bosses once thought to stop elephant charges, on Gujarati-carved or local Bajuni-worked panels. Zanzibar's Stone Town holds hundreds; Lamu's doors favor simpler rectilinear frames. The grammar mixes African, Arabian, Indian and Chinese motifs in one coastal language of welcome and protection.",
